The Biological Constructor Hypothesis: 

A Formal Framework for Generative Genotype–Phenotype Systems and Multiscale Morphological Error Correction

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

Version: 0.7 

AI: chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Gemini 

Abstract

The Biological Constructor Hypothesis (BCH) posits that the genome functions not as a static blueprint but as a critical sub-component within a distributed, self-correcting generative system that produces, maintains, and modifies organismal phenotypes through multiscale dynamical interactions. This framework synthesizes principles from constructor theory, developmental systems theory, biological relativity, and the free energy principle to provide a formal account of morphogenesis as regulated trajectory dynamics toward dynamically encoded manifolds of viable forms.

The central contribution of this work is the Teleodynamic Manifold Decomposition (TMD) framework: a formal treatment of biological construction as multiscale error-correcting regulation of phenotype under perturbation. The TMD framework introduces the concept of a conditional morphogenetic attractor set over stratified Riemannian manifolds with dynamic topology transitions, and characterizes developmental dynamics as non-Euclidean metric contraction toward attractor manifolds.

This manuscript establishes mathematical foundations for the BCH with explicit attention to the distinction between what is proven, what is hypothesized, and what remains speculative. The foundational objective is scientific understanding. Applications in biological intervention are treated as distant implications that cannot be responsibly evaluated until the generative biology is fundamentally understood.

