1. Introduction

1.1 Historical Context

The critique of genetic determinism has a distinguished intellectual history. Developmental Systems Theory (Oyama, 1985) established causal parity between genetic and non-genetic factors. Pigliucci (2010) argued explicitly that the blueprint metaphor should be replaced by a developmental encoding conception, building on Alberch's (1991) dynamical-systems account of genotype–phenotype mapping. Waddington's epigenetic landscape (1957) provided the first formal dynamical-systems representation of development.

Constructor Theory of Life (Marletto, 2015; Deutsch and Marletto, 2015) provides a counterfactual framework in which replication, reproduction, and selection can be formulated in terms of tasks and constructors. The relationship between morphogenesis, shape information, and free-energy minimization has been explicitly proposed by Friston et al. (2015).

1.2 The Central Distinction

Before proceeding, we establish a foundational distinction that structures the entire framework:

\boxed{\text{BCH is a hypothesis about biological organization}}

\boxed{\text{TMD is the mathematical language used to represent and test that hypothesis}}

\boxed{\text{VBC is the computational inference architecture}}

The conceptual hierarchy is:

\text{Biological Constructor Hypothesis} \downarrow

\text{Teleodynamic Manifold Decomposition} \downarrow

\text{Hybrid stochastic dynamical system} \downarrow

\text{Coarse-graining / morphology representation} \downarrow

\text{Attractor + contraction inference} \downarrow

\text{Virtual Biological Constructor}

This distinction prevents conflation between the biological claim (organisms are self-correcting generative systems), the mathematical representation (stratified Riemannian manifolds with contraction dynamics), and the computational implementation (a trainable architecture for inference and prediction).

1.3 The Central Question

\boxed{\text{Does biological organization actively restore the system toward an appropriate attractor following perturbation, and can this restorative dynamics be formalized mathematically and inferred by computational systems?}}

