2. The Biological Constructor: Formal Definition

2.1 Definition

Definition 2.1 (Biological Constructor). A biological constructor is a tuple:

\mathcal{B} = (\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{U}, \mathcal{E}, F, \Phi, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{C})

where:

· \mathcal{X}: multiscale biological state space (stratified Riemannian manifold).
· \mathcal{U}: perturbation/intervention space.
· \mathcal{E}: environmental space.
· F: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{E} \times \mathcal{U} \to \mathcal{X}: transition dynamics.
· \Phi: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{S}: coarse-graining operator mapping micro-states to macro-observables.
· \mathcal{A} = \{A_1, \ldots, A_k\} \subset \mathcal{S}: attractor structure in coarse-grained space.
· \mathcal{C}: causal structure relating interventions to outcomes.

2.2 Constructor-Theoretic Grounding

Established Result (Marletto, 2015). Constructor theory provides a formal framework for characterizing tasks as input-output transformations and constructors as entities capable of performing tasks while retaining the capacity for repetition.

Definition 2.2 (Task Capability). A biological constructor \mathcal{B} is capable of task (x \to y) if, when presented with input state x \in \mathcal{X}, it produces output state y \in \mathcal{X} with probability exceeding threshold \eta, while retaining the capacity to perform the task again.

Derived Proposition 2.3 (Task Composition Closure). If a biological constructor is capable of tasks (x \to y) and (y \to z), then it is capable of the composed task (x \to z).

Proof. This follows from the definition of task capability and the transitivity of dynamical trajectories. If \mathcal{B} remains capable after performing (x \to y), and can then perform (y \to z), sequential application yields (x \to z). \square

2.3 Constraint Closure and Biological Relativity

Established Result (Maturana and Varela, 1980; Mossio and Moreno, 2015). Living systems exhibit constraint closure: each constraint is produced and maintained by the operation of other constraints within the system.

Established Result (Noble, 2006). Biological relativity: there is no privileged level of causation in biological systems.

Derived Proposition 2.4 (No-Privileged-Level Corollary). In a biological constructor satisfying constraint closure, the genome G cannot be the unique causal determinant of phenotype O.

Proof. By constraint closure, the genome G is itself maintained by other constraints C_j within the system. If G were the unique causal determinant of O, then C_j would be determined by G, which is determined by C_j, creating a circular dependence that violates the assumption of unidirectional determination. \square

