3. Mathematical Foundations: Stratified Riemannian Manifolds and Hybrid Stochastic Dynamics

3.1 The State Space as a Stratified Riemannian Manifold

Definition 3.1 (Combinatorial Biological Graph). The biological state space at time t is associated with a combinatorial graph:

\mathcal{H}(t) = (\mathcal{V}(t), \mathcal{E}(t), \mathcal{W}(t))

where \mathcal{V}(t) is the vertex set (cell populations or spatial regions), \mathcal{E}(t) \subseteq \mathcal{V}(t) \times \mathcal{V}(t) is the edge set (signaling or mechanical connections), and \mathcal{W}(t): \mathcal{E}(t) \to \mathbb{R}^{n_w} is the edge weight function with n_w continuous degrees of freedom per edge.

Definition 3.2 (Stratified Riemannian Manifold). The biological state space is a stratified Riemannian manifold \mathcal{X}, decomposed as:

\mathcal{X} = \bigsqcup_{\mathcal{H} \in \mathscr{H}} \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}}

where \mathscr{H} is the family of admissible combinatorial biological graphs, and each stratum \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}} is a smooth Riemannian manifold with dimension:

\dim \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}} = n_x |\mathcal{V}| + n_w |\mathcal{E}| + n_c

where:

· n_x: dimension of the state space of a single cell.
· n_w: dimension of continuous variables associated with each edge.
· n_c: dimension of additional global continuous variables (e.g., morphogen concentrations, mechanical fields).

Justification. The stratified manifold formalism resolves the fundamental limitation of fixed-dimensional Euclidean state spaces: cell division, apoptosis, differentiation, and tissue reorganization continuously change the dimension and topology of the state space. Between discrete events, the system evolves on a fixed stratum \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}}; at event times, it transitions between strata via reset maps.

3.2 Hybrid Dynamical System Formulation

Definition 3.3 (Family of Strata). Define the family of strata indexed by admissible biological graphs:

\mathfrak{X} = \{(\mathcal{H}, \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}}, g_{\mathcal{H}})\}_{\mathcal{H} \in \mathscr{H}}

where g_{\mathcal{H}} is the Riemannian metric on \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}}.

Definition 3.4 (Topological Reset Maps). For each pair of admissible graphs (\mathcal{H}_i, \mathcal{H}_j) connected by a biological event (cell division, apoptosis, fusion), define the reset map:

R_{ij}: \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}_i} \to \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}_j}

Derived Proposition 3.5 (Reset Map Well-Posedness). The reset maps R_{ij} are well-defined as measurable maps between strata under the following conditions:

1. Cell division: R_{ij}(X) = (X, x_{\text{new}}), where x_{\text{new}} is the initial state of the daughter cell, drawn from a specified distribution.
2. Apoptosis: R_{ij}(X) = X_{\setminus v}, removing the state of the apoptotic cell.
3. Cell fusion: R_{ij}(X) = (X_{\setminus v_1, v_2}, x_{\text{fused}}), where x_{\text{fused}} is a function of the fusing cells' states.

Each operation is measurable with respect to the Borel \sigma-algebras on the respective strata. \square

3.3 Stochastic Hybrid Dynamics

Definition 3.6 (Biological Constructor Dynamics). Between reset events (i.e., on a fixed stratum \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}}), the dynamics is governed by a stochastic differential equation:

dX(t) = F_\theta(X(t), E(t), \mathcal{H}) \, dt + \Sigma(X(t), E(t), \mathcal{H}) \, dW_t

where:

· F_\theta: \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}} \to T\mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}}: drift vector field.
· \Sigma: \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}} \to \mathbb{R}^{\dim \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}} \times m}: volatility matrix.
· dW_t: m-dimensional standard Wiener process on \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}}.

At discrete event times \tau_k, reset maps occur:

X(\tau_k^+) = R_{\mathcal{H}_k, \mathcal{H}_{k+1}}(X(\tau_k^-))

Derived Proposition 3.7 (Local Existence and Uniqueness). Under the following assumptions:

1. F_\theta and \Sigma are locally Lipschitz continuous on each stratum \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}}.
2. The reset map R_{ij} is measurable.
3. The sequence of event times \{\tau_k\} has no finite accumulation point almost surely.

the stochastic hybrid system admits a unique strong solution up to a maximal stopping time \zeta.

Proof. Between reset events, the dynamics reduces to a standard SDE on a fixed Riemannian manifold, for which local existence and uniqueness follow from Lipschitz continuity (Hsu, 2002). The reset maps are measurable, preserving the solution's existence across events. The no-finite-accumulation condition ensures the process is well-defined on [0, \zeta). \square

Derived Proposition 3.8 (Global Existence). The solution exists for all t \in [0, T] if, in addition to the assumptions of Proposition 3.7, there exists a Lyapunov function V: \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}_+ such that:

\mathcal{L}V(X) \leq C_1 V(X) + C_2

for constants C_1, C_2 > 0, where \mathcal{L} is the infinitesimal generator of the hybrid process, and V(X(\tau_k^+)) \leq C_3 V(X(\tau_k^-)) for C_3 > 0 at all reset times.

Proof. Standard Lyapunov-based non-explosion criterion for hybrid stochastic systems (Øksendal and Sulem, 2007). \square

3.4 Coarse-Graining and Observability

Definition 3.9 (Coarse-Graining Operator). The coarse-graining operator maps micro-states to macro-observables:

\Phi: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{S}

where \mathcal{S} is the space of macro-level anatomical or phenotypic configurations.

BCH Hypothesis 3.10 (Causal Emergence of Macro-States). For biological constructors, the optimal modeling level for causal inference is not the molecular level but the level at which causal structure is maximally informative—typically the cellular or tissue level.

Rationale. This hypothesis is motivated by the causal emergence framework (Hoel et al., 2013), which proves that macro-level causal models can possess higher effective information than micro-level descriptions.

Derived Proposition 3.11 (Destructive Observability Constraint). Fine-scale measurement of biological state often destroys the system being measured, imposing an observability constraint: simultaneous fine-scale state measurement and longitudinal trajectory tracking of a single biological constructor is generally not possible.

Formal Statement. Let \mathcal{O}_{\text{fine}} denote observables accessible through destructive measurement, and \mathcal{O}_{\text{longitudinal}} denote observables accessible through non-destructive tracking. Then:

\mathcal{O}_{\text{fine}} \cap \mathcal{O}_{\text{longitudinal}} \neq \mathcal{O}_{\text{perfect}}

where \mathcal{O}_{\text{perfect}} denotes the hypothetical complete observable set.

Status. Practical constraint derived from measurement technology limitations, not a mathematical theorem.

