6. Operationalizing the Coarse-Graining Operator \Phi

6.1 Topological Data Analysis Filtration

Definition 6.1 (TDA-Based Coarse-Graining). Define the macro-morphological state S \in \mathcal{S} as a point in a persistence diagram space \mathcal{D}_k, generated by a specified filtration over 3D cell centroid coordinates X_{\text{spatial}}:

\Phi_{\text{TDA}}(X(t)) = \text{PersistenceDiagram}_k(\mathcal{V}(t), \mathcal{W}(t))

where k denotes the homology dimension (e.g., k=1 for loops, k=2 for cavities), and the filtration is explicitly specified (e.g., Vietoris-Rips, distance-to-measure, or alpha complex).

Derived Proposition 6.2 (Conditional TDA Stability). Under the following conditions:

1. The filtration used to compute persistence diagrams is Lipschitz with respect to the input metric.
2. The input metric is the Gromov-Hausdorff distance or a compatible metric.
3. The bottleneck distance d_B is used to compare persistence diagrams.

if two spatial configurations X and X' satisfy d_{GH}(X, X') < \epsilon, then:

d_B(\Phi_{\text{TDA}}(X), \Phi_{\text{TDA}}(X')) < L \epsilon

where L is the Lipschitz constant of the filtration.

Proof. This follows from the stability theorem for persistence diagrams (Cohen-Steiner et al., 2007), applied to the specified filtration. The Lipschitz constant L depends on the choice of filtration and must be determined for each application. \square

Comment on Theorem Selection. The persistence-diagram stability theorem applies to filtrations of functions on fixed spaces. For the Gromov-Hausdorff setting, additional technical conditions are required. This proposition is stated conditionally, with the understanding that the filtration choice must be justified for each biological application.

6.2 Information Bottleneck Coarse-Graining

Definition 6.3 (Information Bottleneck Coarse-Graining). The optimal coarse-graining within a restricted parameterized family is:

\Phi_{\psi^*} = \arg\max_{\psi \in \Psi} \left[ I(\Phi_\psi(X(t)); A_i) - \beta I(X(t); \Phi_\psi(X(t))) \right]

where:

· I(\cdot; \cdot): mutual information.
· \beta: compression trade-off parameter.
· \Psi \subset \mathbb{R}^p: compact parameter domain for the coarse-graining family.

Derived Proposition 6.4 (Existence of Optimal Coarse-Graining in Restricted Family). If:

1. The parameter domain \Psi is compact.
2. The map \psi \mapsto \Phi_\psi is continuous in the appropriate topology.
3. The mutual information terms are continuous in \psi.

then the information bottleneck objective admits at least one optimal solution in \Psi.

Proof. The objective functional is continuous over the compact set \Psi. By the extreme value theorem, the supremum is attained. \square

Comment on Scope. This proposition does not claim existence over the space of all measurable maps, which would require a stronger topology than what is available. The restricted-parameterization formulation is the one used in practice and is mathematically defensible.

