7. The Virtual Biological Constructor: Architecture and Learning

7.1 The Tripartite Model Architecture

BCH Hypothesis 7.1 (Tripartite Architecture). A Virtual Biological Constructor (VBC) should learn three distinct functions:

1. Forward Model F: (G, C, E, U) \to P_{t:t+T}: predicts phenotypic trajectories from genomic, cellular, environmental, and perturbation inputs.
2. Inference Model I: P_{0:T} \to (\hat{C}, \hat{G}, \hat{A}): infers latent states, genomic constraints, and attractor structure from observed trajectories.
3. Intervention Model \Pi: (P, A, E) \to U: proposes perturbations to achieve desired phenotypic outcomes.

The models interact cyclically: F \leftrightarrow I \leftrightarrow \Pi.

7.2 Regularized TMD Loss Function

Definition 7.2 (Regularized TMD Loss Function). The VBC is trained to minimize:

\mathcal{L}_{\text{TMD}} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{reconstruct}} + \lambda_1 \mathcal{L}_{\text{repair}} + \lambda_2 \mathcal{L}_{\text{attractor}} + \lambda_3 \mathcal{L}_{\text{contract}} + \alpha_1 \mathcal{R}_{\text{spec}}(\theta) + \alpha_2 \mathcal{R}_{\text{curv}}(\theta)

where:

· \mathcal{L}_{\text{reconstruct}} = \| \hat{S}(T) - S_{\text{observed}}(T) \|^2: prediction error.
· \mathcal{L}_{\text{repair}} = \| \hat{K}_{\text{repair}} - K_{\text{true}} \|^2: repair kernel fidelity (if known from perturbation data).
· \mathcal{L}_{\text{attractor}} = -\log \| \operatorname{Log}_{\hat{S}(T)}(\hat{A}) \|^2: attractor convergence (geodesic distance).
· \mathcal{L}_{\text{contract}} = \max(0, \lambda_{\max}(J_M) + \beta): contraction constraint.
· \mathcal{R}_{\text{spec}}(\theta): spectral gap regularizer.
· \mathcal{R}_{\text{curv}}(\theta): sectional curvature bound.

Definition 7.3 (Spectral Gap Regularizer). Enforces graph Laplacian spectral gaps on cellular interaction graphs:

\mathcal{R}_{\text{spec}}(\theta) = \| \Delta_{\mathcal{G}} X - \lambda_2 X \|_2^2

where \Delta_{\mathcal{G}} is the graph Laplacian and \lambda_2 is the algebraic connectivity (Fiedler eigenvalue).

Definition 7.4 (Sectional Curvature Bound). Bounds the sectional curvature K_{\mathcal{S}} of the learned macro-state manifold:

\mathcal{R}_{\text{curv}}(\theta) = \max(0, |K_{\mathcal{S}}| - K_{\max})

7.3 Breaking Observational Equivalence

Derived Proposition 7.5 (Observational Equivalence). For any finite dataset of observed trajectories \{O_i(t)\}_{i=1}^N, there exist infinitely many distinct microscopic dynamics \{F_\theta^{(k)}\}_{k=1}^\infty that produce identical macro-scale trajectories.

Proof. The macro-observable map \Phi: \mathcal{X}_{\text{micro}} \to \mathcal{S}_{\text{macro}} is non-injective. For any F_\theta producing O(t), construct F_\theta' that differs on micro-states not in the preimage of the observed trajectory but agrees on the preimage. \square

Derived Proposition 7.6 (Regularization Reduces Equivalence Class). The spectral gap and curvature regularizers constrain the space of admissible dynamics, reducing (though not eliminating) observational equivalence by excluding physically implausible solutions.

Proof Sketch. The regularizers impose constraints beyond trajectory matching: they require the learned dynamics to respect spatial smoothness (spectral gap) and geometric well-behavedness (curvature bound). These constraints exclude the arbitrary perturbations used in the proof of Proposition 7.5 that violate smoothness or introduce artificial singularities. The remaining equivalence class is strictly smaller, though not necessarily a singleton. \square

Comment on Scope. The degree of reduction in equivalence class is an empirical question. The claim here is qualitative: regularization reduces ambiguity, but the extent of reduction depends on the specific regularizers and their hyperparameters.

