Appendix A: Proofs for Section 2

A.1 Proof of Proposition 2.3 (Task Composition Closure)

Statement. If a biological constructor is capable of tasks (x \to y) and (y \to z), then it is capable of the composed task (x \to z).

Proof. By Definition 2.2, capability of task (x \to y) means that when presented with input state x, the constructor produces output state y with probability at least \eta, and after doing so retains the capacity to perform the task again. Similarly, capability of (y \to z) means that presented with y, it produces z with probability at least \eta and retains capacity.

Now consider the composite input x. By capability of (x \to y), the constructor transforms x to y with probability p_1 \ge \eta. After this transformation, the constructor is still capable, so it can perform (y \to z) on the resulting state y, producing z with probability p_2 \ge \eta, conditional on having produced y. The total probability of producing z from x is at least p_1 p_2 \ge \eta^2. If we require a threshold \eta' for the composite task, we may set \eta' = \eta^2 (or adjust the definition to require a fixed threshold with repeated attempts). The constructor retains capacity after the composite task by concatenation of capacity retention. Thus the composed task (x \to z) is performable. \square

A.2 Proof of Proposition 2.4 (No-Privileged-Level Corollary)

Statement. In a biological constructor satisfying constraint closure, the genome G cannot be the unique causal determinant of phenotype O.

Proof. Let \mathcal{C} = \{C_1, C_2, \dots, C_n\} be the set of constraints in the system, including the genome G (say C_1 = G). Constraint closure (Maturana & Varela, 1980; Mossio & Moreno, 2015) asserts that every constraint C_i is produced and maintained by the operation of other constraints in \mathcal{C}. In particular, G is produced and maintained by some subset \mathcal{C}_G \subseteq \mathcal{C} \setminus \{G\}.

Assume for contradiction that G is the unique causal determinant of O. That means O is a function of G alone: O = f(G), and no other C_i directly influences O. However, since G is maintained by \mathcal{C}_G, the state of G itself depends on those other constraints: G = h(\mathcal{C}_G). Then O = f(h(\mathcal{C}_G)), so O is causally dependent on \mathcal{C}_G as well. This contradicts the uniqueness of G as determinant. Therefore G cannot be the unique causal determinant. \square

