Appendix B: Proofs for Section 3

B.1 Proof of Proposition 3.5 (Reset Map Well-Posedness)

Statement. The reset maps R_{ij}: \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}_i} \to \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}_j} are well-defined as measurable maps under the specified conditions.

Proof. We consider each type of biological event:

1. Cell division (split): \mathcal{H}_i has vertex set \mathcal{V}_i, and \mathcal{H}_j is obtained by adding one new vertex v_{\text{new}} and possibly edges. The reset map is R_{ij}(X) = (X, x_{\text{new}}), where x_{\text{new}} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_x} is the initial state of the daughter cell. The map is defined on all of \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}_i} and is continuous (hence measurable) because it is essentially the identity on existing coordinates and a constant (or random draw from a fixed distribution) on the new coordinates. If x_{\text{new}} is random (e.g., drawn from a distribution depending on the mother cell state), then R_{ij} is a measurable Markov kernel, still well-defined.
2. Apoptosis (death): \mathcal{H}_i has vertex v that is removed. The reset map R_{ij}(X) = X_{\setminus v} is the projection removing the coordinates corresponding to cell v. This is a continuous linear projection, hence measurable.
3. Cell fusion (merge): Two vertices v_1, v_2 are replaced by a new vertex v_{\text{fused}}. The reset map is R_{ij}(X) = (X_{\setminus \{v_1,v_2\}}, x_{\text{fused}}) where x_{\text{fused}} = \psi(x_{v_1}, x_{v_2}) for some measurable function \psi (e.g., averaging). This is measurable as composition of measurable functions.

Thus all reset maps are measurable. \square

B.2 Proof of Proposition 3.7 (Local Existence and Uniqueness)

Statement. Under assumptions (1) F_\theta and \Sigma locally Lipschitz on each stratum, (2) reset maps measurable, (3) no finite accumulation of event times a.s., the hybrid system admits a unique strong solution up to a maximal stopping time.

Proof. We proceed by induction on event times. Let \tau_0 = 0, X(0) = X_0, and \mathcal{H}_0 given. Between \tau_0 and the first event time \tau_1, the dynamics is governed by the SDE on the fixed stratum \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}_0}:

dX(t) = F_\theta(X(t), E(t), \mathcal{H}_0) dt + \Sigma(X(t), E(t), \mathcal{H}_0) dW_t.

Since F_\theta and \Sigma are locally Lipschitz on \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}_0}, standard results for SDEs on Riemannian manifolds (Hsu, 2002, Theorem 3.1.1) guarantee the existence of a unique strong solution up to a stopping time \zeta_1 (which may be infinite if no explosion). Because the coefficients are defined globally on the stratum (but may fail global Lipschitz), the solution exists up to a maximal time \zeta_1 at which it may escape to infinity or hit the boundary of the stratum.

The event time \tau_1 is defined as the first time a biological event occurs (cell division, death, fusion). The event process is assumed to have intensity that is bounded and state-dependent, but by assumption (3) event times do not accumulate in finite time. Therefore \tau_1 is finite or infinite. If \tau_1 < \zeta_1, the solution exists up to \tau_1. At \tau_1, we apply the reset map R_{\mathcal{H}_0,\mathcal{H}_1} to X(\tau_1^-) to obtain X(\tau_1^+). By Proposition 3.5, this is measurable and yields a new state in \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}_1}.

Now we restart the process with initial condition X(\tau_1^+) on stratum \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{H}_1}. By the same argument, a unique solution exists until the next event time \tau_2 or explosion. Since event times do not accumulate, we can piece together these solutions to obtain a unique strong solution up to a maximal stopping time \zeta = \min(\lim_{k\to\infty} \tau_k, \zeta_{\max}) where \zeta_{\max} is the first explosion time. \square

B.3 Proof of Proposition 3.8 (Global Existence)

Statement. Under additional Lyapunov condition, the solution exists for all t \in [0,T].

Proof. We use a stochastic Lyapunov argument. Let V: \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}_+ be a Lyapunov function satisfying the conditions:

(i) \mathcal{L}V(X) \leq C_1 V(X) + C_2 between events, where \mathcal{L} is the infinitesimal generator of the SDE on each stratum.
(ii) V(X(\tau_k^+)) \leq C_3 V(X(\tau_k^-)) at reset times.

We show that \mathbb{E}[V(X(t))] is bounded on any finite interval [0,T]. Between events, by Itô's formula and condition (i), we have

\mathbb{E}[V(X(t \wedge \tau_{k+1}))] \leq \mathbb{E}[V(X(\tau_k^+))] e^{C_1 (t-\tau_k)} + \frac{C_2}{C_1}(e^{C_1 (t-\tau_k)} - 1).

Using condition (ii), after each reset we multiply by at most C_3. Since the number of events in [0,T] is finite a.s. (no accumulation), we can iterate to get

\mathbb{E}[V(X(t))] \leq C_T (V(X_0) + 1)

for some constant C_T. By Markov's inequality, \sup_{t \in [0,T]} V(X(t)) < \infty a.s. Since V is proper (tends to infinity as state escapes to infinity), this precludes explosion. Hence solution exists globally. \square

B.4 Remark on Proposition 3.11 (Destructive Observability Constraint)

This proposition is not a mathematical theorem but a practical constraint derived from measurement technology. The formal statement is given, but no proof is required. It is included as a derived proposition in the sense of a logical consequence of the definitions of \mathcal{O}_{\text{fine}} and \mathcal{O}_{\text{longitudinal}} and the fact that fine-scale measurement often destroys the sample. The intersection is not equal to the perfect observable set because some observables require destructive measurement and cannot be obtained longitudinally. \square

