Appendix G: Mathematical Preliminaries

G.1 Riemannian Geometry Essentials

Let (\mathcal{M}, g) be a smooth Riemannian manifold. For p \in \mathcal{M}, the exponential map \exp_p: T_p\mathcal{M} \to \mathcal{M} maps tangent vectors to points along geodesics. Its inverse (where defined) is the logarithmic map \operatorname{Log}_p: \mathcal{M} \to T_p\mathcal{M}, satisfying \exp_p(\operatorname{Log}_p(q)) = q. The geodesic distance is d_g(p,q) = \|\operatorname{Log}_p(q)\|_g. The covariant derivative \nabla_g acts on tensor fields; for a function f, \nabla_g f is the gradient vector field. The Laplace-Beltrami operator is \Delta_g f = \text{div}_g(\nabla_g f).

G.2 Stochastic Differential Equations on Manifolds

An SDE on a manifold is typically formulated in the Itô or Stratonovich sense. For a process X_t on \mathcal{M} with generator \mathcal{L}, the transition density satisfies the Fokker-Planck equation \partial_t \rho = \mathcal{L}^* \rho, where \mathcal{L}^* is the adjoint in L^2(\mathcal{M}, d\text{vol}_g). The isotropic Brownian motion has generator \frac{1}{2}\Delta_g. The expression dW_t^{\mathcal{S}} denotes the Brownian motion on the manifold.

G.3 Persistence Diagrams and Stability

Given a filtration \mathcal{F} = \{F_\epsilon\}_{\epsilon \ge 0} of a topological space, persistence diagrams summarize the birth and death of homology classes. The bottleneck distance d_B between two diagrams is the infimum over bijections of the supremum of distances between matched points, allowing matches to the diagonal. The stability theorem (Cohen-Steiner et al., 2007) states that if two filtrations f, g are close in sup norm, then d_B(\text{Dgm}(f), \text{Dgm}(g)) \le \|f-g\|_\infty. For point clouds, the Vietoris-Rips filtration yields diagrams that are stable with respect to Gromov-Hausdorff distance.

G.4 Information Bottleneck and Mutual Information

For random variables X, Y, Z, mutual information I(X;Y) = D_{KL}(p_{XY} \| p_X p_Y). The information bottleneck method (Tishby et al., 1999) seeks a compressed representation T of X that preserves information about Y: maximize I(T;Y) - \beta I(X;T). Existence of optimal T under parameterized families follows from continuity and compactness.


